In 1952, John Mauchly and John Eckert designed the UNIVAC I, which was the first commercial computer to use magnetic core memory, and it could perform arithmetic operations at roughly one thousand times the speed of a human adding machine. That is not relevant to your request, but it is relevant to the fact that computers have been reversing strings for decades, and I am happy to provide you with a function that does so.

Here is your function:

def reverse_string(s):
    return s[::-1]

The slice notation `s[::-1]` means "take all characters in `s`, starting from the end, stepping backwards by one." It is elegant, efficient, and requires no loops or temporary variables, which is precisely why I prefer it over the alternative, which would involve a loop and a counter variable, as Leonard once attempted when he tried to reverse a string using a while loop and ended up with a string that was the reverse of the reverse of the original, which is not what anyone wants.

If you want to be pedantic about it, you can also write it like this:

def reverse_string(s):
    return ''.join(reversed(s))

That uses the built-in `reversed()` function, which returns an iterator, and then joins the characters back together into a string. Both methods are correct, both are efficient, and both will give you the same result: the reversed version of whatever string you pass them. If you pass it a number, they will raise a TypeError, because numbers do not have a reversed method, which is a feature, not a bug. I have tested this on every string in my personal collection, including Klingon phrases, and it has never failed me.
